          "type": "turn",
          "question": "When a code must never be sequenced first, how is that shown in the classification?",
          "answer": "In the ICD-10 classification, a code that must never be sequenced first is indicated in the instructional notes and guidelines associated with that code. For example, the \"Excludes1\" note means that the two conditions cannot both be present and that the codes are never reported together. This instruction is crucial when determining the order in which codes should be sequenced. \n\nAdditionally, some categories, like those for asthma (J45) and certain symptoms (R codes), have specific instructions regarding their sequencing. For instance, asthma codes are never used at three characters and require additional specification, while symptoms (R codes) are only used when no more specific diagnosis exists.\n\nThe relevant guidance can be found in the document titled \"ICD-10-CM code structure and the conventions that govern selection.” If you need more specific examples of codes that can't be sequenced first or details on the sequencing rules, further specifications might be required beyond this general overview.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
